    "q-345097": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345097",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes.",
      "worked_solution": "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{42}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{144}{25})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
