      "source_example_id": "e-21891",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF",
      "kp_id": "e-21891"
    },
    "q-345129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345129",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14185497/assets/f5c38b340f0e64375d0e163bddaf194cba4bf11f4ef123d3a717fac72e2da94f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n5(0)+20(0)+5q=10,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n5q=10\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $2$ coulombs.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
